#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343599 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "00cf6180-070b-5ebf-af24-bc4c4b0bd94e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvector of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fec7827-bad7-547f-8389-4e4d0e10f480" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{3}{5}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343601} {:answer/id #uuid "07c591b3-2963-54f9-b371-6bcff25ea2d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{13}-\\frac{3}{13}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343602} {:answer/id #uuid "97bc9eb9-99fb-5f8a-bec6-4f6938aedf5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2-3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343603} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce75948-bf18-5d34-9b4b-899ae78011ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343604} {:answer/id #uuid "29721852-3731-5992-bbbd-6aff9aebc555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{13}+\\frac{3}{13}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29721852-3731-5992-bbbd-6aff9aebc555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{13}+\\frac{3}{13}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343605} :answer-field/id #uuid "a028c170-ff03-5d7a-a774-14680687814a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "44d22880-1719-5f05-b9a2-8b81c0330bc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-81030" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 13 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1+3\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-1+3\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 13 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2-3\\text{i} & -1 \\\\ 13 & 2-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-2-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 13{x}_{1}+(2-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-1+3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-2-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -(2+3\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=-1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-1}{2+3\\text{i}}=\\frac{-(2-3\\text{i})}{13}=-\\frac{2}{13}+\\frac{3}{13}\\text{i}.$"}]}
